# Engaging in conflict to make decisions together

##### NGL helps post-growth business Greaterthan work through a stuck conflict and make difficult decisions together using Convergent Facilitation

## Introduction

A challenge that emerges in experimenting with collaborative ways of
functioning is: *what do we do when we are in conflict and can't find a
way to make a decision together?* In 2025 and 2026, members of
Nonviolent Global Liberation (NGL) supported Greaterthan (GT) to create
movement after a period of stuckness coming from a challenging conflict
between two founding partners. GT invited Miki Kashtan, through whom NGL
came into being in 2017, to support GT in moving forward. Miki invited a
group of people within NGL to apprentice with her and steward the
project. [Miki facilitated:]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

-   [First, a conflict process to restore
    trust]{style="font-weight: 400;"}
-   [Then, a Convergent Facilitation process to make a decision
    together]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

## [Background]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

[[GT]{style="font-weight: 400;"}](https://greaterthan.works/)[ is a
collectively governed, post-growth business that has been experimenting
for over a decade with shifting systems and supporting other
organizations to do the same, as a way of facing the increasingly
complex and interconnected challenges of our world, which requires
collective action. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}
[[NGL]{style="font-weight: 400;"}](https://nglcommunity.org/)[ is a
community of people passionate about closing the gap between our current
global crises and fully interdependent living. Within NGL, we experiment
and apply organizational systems and structures to bring people together
to make complex decisions in an integrative way, build capacity for
collaborative leadership, and, all in all, find ways to anchor vision in
how they function -- even within the world as it
is.]{style="font-weight: 400;"} [The conflict in GT was significant:
some members even wanted to dissolve GT and start something new. NGL
brought in the core premise of ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}[[Convergent
Facilitation]{style="font-weight: 400;"}](https://convergentfacilitation.org/)[:
if it is possible to make a decision that works for all, it is
preferable over a decision that works for some. With Miki facilitating,
the GT members were all willing to try it and see where it took them,
and we agreed that some groundwork to restore enough trust to make a
decision together would take place first.]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

# [Engaging with the conflict ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

[We -- Miki and those in NGL -- believed that before GT could come
together to make decisions about how to move forward, actively engaging
with the conflict was necessary. We were not aiming to 'resolve' the
conflict, but to increase collective trust, and support the people who
were in conflict to act from choice rather than reaction, thus enabling
them to be able to actually make a decision
together.]{style="font-weight: 400;"} [We look at the source of a
conflict as the gap between both people's narratives about what
happened. And in order to attend to this gap in people's understanding,
and restore enough trust to allow for the CF process, we supported GT
members through a process of information gathering, drafting a
noncontroversial conflict narrative and acknowledgements, and sharing
this with the wider GT community to restore
trust.]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

## [Gathering Information]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

[We began by collecting information from everyone -- including the
people primarily involved, as well as the acutely impacted parties in
the field where the conflict was happening:]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

-   [Gathering any information that would be relevant for weaving
    togetherness in the field. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}
-   [Crafting agreements together with them to care for impacts that
    might continue to happen, so that those needed in the
    decision-making process might find it more possible to work together
    during the process -- whether directly or
    indirectly.]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

## [Drafting a Conflict Narrative ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

[The purpose of a conflict narrative is for the people involved in the
conflict to arrive at a ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[shared
reality,]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[ even while their versions of what
happened diverge.]{style="font-weight: 400;"} [We started by documenting
the narratives that the people in the conflict were initially carrying.
To gather information for the conflict narrative, we had multiple
calls:]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

-   [With each of the two individuals in the conflict, separately: what
    would you say is happening / is the reason for the
    conflict? ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}
-   [With those acutely impacted in the conflict field: what makes you
    acutely impacted in this conflict? If the two people in the conflict
    came to you for advice, what would you propose to address the
    situation?]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

[In order to have a shared starting point to see what\'s happening and
what led to the conflict, the conflict narrative needs to be
]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[noncontroversial]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[.
For us, this involved explicitly naming opinions and interpretations as
what someone thinks instead of presenting them as truth. For example,
instead of 'what person A did was dismissive', clearly attributing the
interpretation to an individual: 'person B experienced what person A did
as dismissive'. This supports everyone involved to be able to accept the
overall narrative.]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

## [Adding Acknowledgments ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

[After drafting the narrative of each of the two parties, we shared it
with each of them individually and accompanied them to digest it.
Although it can be painful to hear another\'s narrative, this is a
pathway for having a shared reality about what is going
on.]{style="font-weight: 400;"} [The next step was collecting and adding
acknowledgements and acknowledgement requests. We did this by connecting
with the two parties individually, and documenting their responses to
two questions:]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

-   [What do you think is something you did that contributed to the
    conflict?]{style="font-weight: 400;"}
-   [What would you like to hear from the other
    person?]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

[This supported us to see where people had a similar story of the
conflict, and where there were gaps between their
understandings. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

-   [Example of acknowledgment request: Person A would have liked Person
    B to name their part in getting them to the conflict and the dynamic
    of stuckness, 'I sometimes get the impression they see this as being
    of my doing alone.']{style="font-weight: 400;"}
-   [Example of acknowledgment: Person A acknowledged that their sharing
    a different experience of past events may be jarring to Person B, 'I
    can understand how my coming in years later saying this must have
    felt very invalidating.']{style="font-weight: 400;"}

## [Weaving togetherness in the conflict]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

[Up until this point, the two primary parties in the conflict had not
interacted with each other about the narrative. Now, Miki facilitated
four conversations with the two of them, not to resolve the conflict,
but to land together in it -- and all of its divergences -- from a place
of togetherness. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"} [Miki supported them to
see the needs and impacts
]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[beneath]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[
their narratives and stories, helping both
]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[reframe]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[ the
story and see the different ways they were making sense of the same
situation. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

-   [When Miki had a sense that someone was stuck in their narrative,
    she checked for concrete examples of what they meant. This helped
    pull them out of their narratives and focus on what actually
    happened, separating observation from
    interpretation. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}
-   [When they spoke about the other person, she checked if it was said
    explicitly, and supported them to own their interpretation of what
    was actually said. For example, reframing from 'this person excluded
    me' to 'I interpreted this action as
    exclusion.']{style="font-weight: 400;"}

[Miki also moved them through specific
]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[alignments and divergences
]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[in their Conflict Narratives. Asking: What
hasn't been spoken that needs to be spoken, before making decisions
together? This phase helped to clear remaining important points and move
towards restoring trust.]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

-   [First, naming pieces that were true for everyone was a source of
    energy, revealing where there is already some
    alignment. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}
-   [Second, when the parties shared divergences and checked if the
    other saw the divergence in the same way, it was a 'yes' to doing
    something together, another source of
    energy. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}
-   [Finally, making visible that, while not everyone may agree about
    the content of the divergences, they could still hold together the
    fact that a divergence
    ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[exists]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[,
    was settling for the field overall. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

[Miki helped them to share ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[acknowledgments
and agreements ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[with each other. This
included understanding the narrative from the angle of: What is useful
from how the other person was experiencing the situation, whether or not
it's true, for making decisions together? This resulted in agreements
based on what they have learned through the conflict narrative. For
example, for the pattern of one person experiencing being excluded, an
agreement was:]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

-   [Whenever Person A becomes aware of the possibility that something
    they did might impact Person B to experience exclusion, they
    acknowledge the potential impact, mourn it, or inquire if not
    sure. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}
-   [Whenever Person B imagines that there may be exclusion going on,
    they ask if it's there, with openness to the possibility that their
    interpretation may be inaccurate.]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

## [Digesting the conflict with all members]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

[At the end of the conflict engagement phase, we shared the conflict
narrative with the broader GT group in two calls, during which everyone
was invited to speak and listen in relation to three
questions:]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

-   [What is still not clear to you from this document about what led to
    the conflict?]{style="font-weight: 400;"}
-   [What are you discovering for the first time that is surprising or
    impactful for you?]{style="font-weight: 400;"}
-   [Are there any insights that arise for you that you think could
    support the field?]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

[These digestion calls had several effects for the broader
group:]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

-   [All impacted members were able to take in new information that
    wasn't visible before about what challenges and narratives led to
    the conflict. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}
-   [They were able to share together in the sadness and compassion for
    the stuckness that was affecting the whole organization up to that
    point.]{style="font-weight: 400;"}
-   [They reflected and expressed lessons, ideas and wishes for how to
    move the next phase forward as a united
    whole.]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

[Following the weaving and digestion calls -- when the two parties were
more connected with the information beneath their stories and could own
their narratives, and the broader community had processed the lessons
from the conflict -- we sensed that the conflict engagement phase could
be closed, and we could move to the next phase of decision making. This
didn't mean that the conflict was 'resolved' or that the two parties
could work together again, only that they had sufficient trust in each
other to hold their considerations and to work toward decisions that
could work for all. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

# [Convergent Facilitation (CF) Process]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

[The main purpose of engaging with the conflict was to create enough
trust and willingness to make some key decisions together about the
future of GT. Once sufficient trust was established, we started a
]{style="font-weight: 400;"}[[Convergent
Facilitation]{style="font-weight: 400;"}](https://convergentfacilitation.org/)[
(CF) process: a collaborative decision-making process designed to build
togetherness across differences by integrating what's important to
everyone involved. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"} [When in conflict, it is
usually hard to trust that ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[solutions that
work for everyone]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[ are possible. Part of
our task was to remind participants that our intention was to find a
solution that works for everyone, to trust that it was possible, and to
hold the process in a way that moved us in that
direction.]{style="font-weight: 400;"} [As GT had faced challenges and
stuckness with the conflict, they had not been able to make some
decisions together with enough cohesion to move GT forward. They also
faced a challenge common in groups that attempt to move out of
hierarchical structures: without the push of hierarchy and coercion, and
in the absence of clear pathways to continue moving their purpose
forward when conflict arises, people didn\'t know how to maintain the
movement, and energy got stuck.]{style="font-weight: 400;"} [The
decisions they were facing were significant, because they were related
to whether and how the organization might continue. We started the CF
process with this question: ']{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[How can GT
move forward, in the context of the conflict, from a place that centers
their values, orients toward the world they are hoping to steward into
being, and seeks to meet as many needs as
possible?]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[' They chose this broader
question, instead of a narrower one like 'will GT continue as an
organization?', to invite creativity, to open up space for ideas of what
might be possible to move forward from an alignment with
values. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"} [The CF process involved three
distinct phases, in which we started with identifying and gathering
noncontroversial criteria, then moved to creating proposals based on
those criteria, and then came together to review the proposals and make
the decision together.]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

## [Phase 1: Gathering Criteria]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

**Purpose**[. This first phase invited the group to shift away from a
common pattern of orienting to divergences and conflict that focuses on
strategies and debate, to instead focus on needs. In many modern
societies, it is common to have discussions in which each person tries
to convince others that their idea is best. Our approach, and the
approach built into CF as a process, was different: before getting into
any concrete proposals or strategies, we guided the group to first focus
on ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[what was important for everyone
involved]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[. This orientation and the
resulting list of criteria could then form the basis from which
creativity could emerge around specific strategies about how to care for
all that was important for each person. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}
**Process.** [This first phase of gathering criteria formed the basis of
trust and coherence for the rest of the CF process. A list of criteria
captured the 'why' underneath people's ideas, opinions, and suggestions:
in total, we supported the group to surface 42 criteria which summarized
and captured fully
]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[what]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[ was
important for people in relation to the decision, while completely
releasing, for now, any thought about
]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[how]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[ to
attend to those criteria.]{style="font-weight: 400;"} [Trusting that the
conflict as a whole was giving the organization relevant feedback about
what needed to be addressed, we started this phase by gathering criteria
from the previous engagements. Three further meetings helped to finalize
the list.]{style="font-weight: 400;"} **Participant feedback.** [A
participant shared: 'I have a feeling that so many interconnected things
are happening in the background that led us to this conflict. I'm happy
we're looking at them all, not just at the surface level.' This showed
how a conflict -- which seemed to be just at the level of relationship
\-- was actually pointing to something bigger within the organization
and revealing voids in their functioning.]{style="font-weight: 400;"}
**Integration**[. In this phase, we witnessed the mystery and power of
integration: when someone raised a concern about a criterion that
someone else proposed, and we integrated it in framing the criterion,
the original person who proposed the criterion was happier. For example,
when someone shared that they didn\'t like a criterion about 'nodes in
different continents', we focused our attention on what was important
for that person -- leaving room for doing things separately and together
-- and then changed the criterion accordingly, to 'care for the
complexities that arise in relation to having members in different
continents'. The new version of the criterion was more robust because it
integrated more wisdom. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

## [Phase 2: Creating Proposals]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

**Purpose**[. In the second phase of CF, the idea is to come up with one
or more proposals that would attend to the list of criteria from
Phase 1. We invited creativity around finding strategies that would care
for all the criteria in order to find a solution that would work for
everyone. It required trusting in the group's collective capacity to
navigate complex situations and allowed the creativity and integrative
wisdom of humans coming together to emerge. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}
**Challenges**[. As is often the case with CF, the second phase was more
complex and it took us two months to arrive at a pathway that could move
the proposal-creation process forward. GT members were used to making
decisions in a certain way that is different from CF, and they had a
tendency to come back to this habitual way of functioning, repeating the
same impacts that brought them to the conflict and the stuckness. We
created integrative pathways that maintained the integrity of the CF
process while keeping some aspects of their current
functioning. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"} **Process**[. We started by
creating committees -- small groups entrusted to create proposals --
which would then be evaluated in relation to the criteria in Phase 3. We
tried different configurations of committees until we landed on having a
single group that was entrusted by everyone to create a
proposal. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"} [The final committee was formed
of three people who:]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

-   [still had the energy and space for creativity to find integrative
    pathways;]{style="font-weight: 400;"}
-   [had enough divergences between them to sufficiently represent
    everyone\'s needs and, from there, to sense into what might work for
    all; and]{style="font-weight: 400;"}
-   [had capacity and willingness to interview a major stakeholder, who
    was not part of the group, to gather information, care as much as
    possible for what was important for that person, and also care for
    what was important for others.]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

## [Phase 3: Review and Decision]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

**Purpose**[. In the third phase, all GT members were convened to see if
the created proposal cared sufficiently for the criteria, so that
everyone would be willing to accept it. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

-   [In particular, this phase is different from other decision-making
    processes like consent or consensus, which focus on asking for
    agreement, in that in the CF process we
    ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[actively look for
    dissent]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}
-   [Also, this phase uses an entire range of
    ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[possible
    thresholds]{style="font-weight: 400;"}*[ -- from "significant
    concern" to "even the smallest doubt" -- to invite dissent that is
    relevant to purpose and capacity within the
    group.]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

**Process**[. All GT members were asked -- over messages and in live
meetings -- to evaluate the proposal in relation to the criteria.
Several elements contributed to a smooth review and decision
phase:]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

-   [Proposals were sent to all members via the GT Slack channel, not
    requiring them to connect to a new channel or call, making it easy
    for all relevant parties to review ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}
-   [The request was specific: inviting concerns, rather than
    preferences ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}
-   [The request was also integrative: if anyone had a concern, they
    were asked to point out the place where they believed the proposal
    needed to be changed or tweaked in order to work for
    everyone]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

**Outcome**[. Once we had all the concerns, we engaged with them one by
one to integrate what people named as missing or controversial until we
landed on a proposal that worked for everyone -- which then became a
decision. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"} [The decision was to change the
shape of GT by dividing it into separate nodes, which GT shared in a
]{style="font-weight: 400;"}[[newsletter]{style="font-weight: 400;"}](https://greaterthan.activehosted.com/social/a8e864d04c95572d1aece099af852d0a.681)[
and
]{style="font-weight: 400;"}[[followup]{style="font-weight: 400;"}](https://greaterthan.activehosted.com/social/fc8001f834f6a5f0561080d134d53d29.684)[.
The proposal included nine sections that detailed specific agreements
about the transition phase and about the relation between
nodes.]{style="font-weight: 400;"} [We celebrate that -- in the midst of
conflict, divergences, and the initial individual pull from some members
to close GT in a faster way -- we were able to move through a six-month
process, with patience, until arriving at a decision that worked for
all. ]{style="font-weight: 400;"}

